3. Roth `$` span, /whatif Roth Conversion card, dark mode (roth-conversion.html:48;
   ACCESSIBILITY.md A-3 "4.5:1 for body text", A-2 "both themes"; WCAG 1.4.3
   Contrast Minimum) — FAIL.
   Measured via real DOM after a genuine #theme-toggle click: span
   getComputedStyle.color = rgb(168,162,158) = #a8a29e (dark:text-gray-400,
   i.e. stone-400 per this project's tailwind.config.js gray→stone override).
   The span is absolutely positioned inside the input's own box and paints
   over the input's actual fill; input getComputedStyle.backgroundColor =
   rgb(68,64,60) = #44403c (dark:bg-gray-700 = stone-700). Font-size
   14.875px, weight 400 — NOT large text (needs ≥18.66px bold / 24px), so
   the 4.5:1 threshold applies, not 3:1.
   Contrast(#a8a29e, #44403c) = 4.07:1 — below 4.5:1.
   Cross-checked independently: (a) hand blend/luminance math in Python
   gives the identical 4.073; (b) a cropped screenshot of the input
   (roth_dark_wrap.png) shows the "$" visibly dimmer than the adjacent
   "50000" value text — legible but measurably substandard, not a false
   positive from stacking-context confusion.
   Provenance: `git diff master -- web/templates/components/whatif/roth-conversion.html`
   shows this task added `dark:text-gray-400` where no dark: variant
   existed before (master used `text-gray-600` = #57534e in all themes,
   which contrasts 1.35:1 against the same dark input bg — effectively
   invisible). BL4 is a real improvement (1.35 -> 4.07) but stops short of
   the BL-RUN-SPEC's own acceptance criterion for this exact line: "Roth
   `$` span: ... ≥ 4.5:1 in dark". This is BL4's own new content, not
   inherited from master or a foreign in-flight task — squarely in scope.
   Axe did NOT catch this: axe's color-contrast rule exempts the lone "$"
   glyph under its `shortTextContent` heuristic (visible in the incomplete
   list for other single-glyph/short SVG text on the same page) — exactly
   the kind of miss ACCESSIBILITY.md's manual-check requirement (and this
   checker's own charter) exists for. A `dark:text-gray-300` (#d6d3d1,
   confirmed elsewhere on this same page at 10.18:1 against the same
   #292524-family dark surfaces) or equivalent would clear 4.5:1; the
   exact replacement token vs. #44403c specifically needs re-measuring by
   whoever fixes this, not assumed by me.

